@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: EXPERIMENT — should `proveo run <agent>` return to the SAME clone each time, instead of a fresh one per run?
' status: proposed 2026-09-07, not implemented; nothing in src depends on this file
' would touch: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (PreserveClone, liftClonedOutput)
' would touch: internal/sbx/run.go (CloneRemote, CloneRefs, CloneFetchArgs), internal/run/run.go (decideClone)
'   the mechanism today .......... _spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml
'   the resume state it rhymes with _spec/internal/sbx/state-sync.puml
'   what a host can carry ........ _spec/minimum_requirements.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE OBSERVATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Clone mode is per-RUN. Every `proveo run cursor` clones the checkout into a
' new sandbox, and at teardown the work comes back as refs/proveo/<sid>/* plus a
' lifted output dir. The sid carries a unix timestamp and a pid, so the second
' run of the day shares nothing with the first: not the branch the agent was
' working on, not its build cache, not its node_modules, not the agent's own
' session history.
'
' For a one-shot task that is exactly right. For the way an agent is actually
' used — several passes at the same change over an afternoon — it means every
' pass starts by rebuilding a world the previous pass already had, and the
' operator reconciles N ref namespaces for one piece of work.
'
' ── WHAT REUSE WOULD BUY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
' A clone keyed by (repository, target) rather than by sid:
'
'   * the agent resumes ON ITS OWN BRANCH, mid-change, with a dirty tree it
'     recognises — instead of a pristine checkout it must re-orient in
'   * the toolchain cache survives: the go build cache, the pnpm store, cargo's
'     registry, whatever `mise` provisioned. On the polyglot sample this is the
'     dominant cost of a cold start
'   * ONE ref namespace per piece of work, so `git log main..refs/proveo/...`
'     answers "what has this agent done" rather than "what did run #7 do"
'   * the harness's own session history stays where the harness left it, which
'     is the thing `sbx` resume state already tries to preserve separately
'
' ── WHAT IT WOULD COST, AND WHY THIS IS AN EXPERIMENT AND NOT A PLAN ───────
' Four questions, none of them settled. Any one of them can sink the idea.
'
' 1. STALENESS. The host checkout moves under the reused clone — the operator
'    commits, rebases, switches branch. A clone that is not refreshed is a
'    silent fork; one that is refreshed unasked can throw away the agent's
'    uncommitted work. Neither is acceptable as a default, and "ask" is a prompt
'    in front of every run.
'
' 2. WHOSE CLONE IS IT. Two `proveo run cursor` in the same repo at once is
'    ordinary (two scopes, two branches). A clone keyed by repo+target makes
'    them collide, so the key needs a third component the operator chooses —
'    at which point it is a named workspace and should be called one.
'
' 3. WHAT MAKES IT SAFE TO DROP. The current guarantee is strong and simple:
'    the sandbox is ephemeral, so nothing accumulates and nothing is left to
'    clean up. A durable clone is state with no lifecycle — it grows, it holds
'    the agent's secrets in its own git config and caches, and `proveo clean`
'    would need to know about it. See minimum_requirements: a stopped sandbox
'    still holds its disk volumes.
'
' 4. IS IT EVEN PROVEO'S TO KEEP? sbx 0.42 grew `sbx env` — an environment file
'    that describes a named sandbox, its workspaces, credentials and lifecycle
'    commands, and reuses it across invocations. That is very close to this
'    idea, one layer down, maintained by the backend. Building a second
'    mechanism above it would be proveo owning state sbx already owns.
'
' ── THE CHEAPEST EXPERIMENT ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Do not implement reuse. MEASURE the thing reuse is supposed to buy, because
' the whole case rests on cold-start cost being large:
'
'   a. on e2e/samples, time three consecutive `proveo run <agent>` runs and
'      record where the wall clock goes — clone, toolchain provisioning, the
'      agent's own orientation
'   b. repeat with a hand-made durable clone (`--clone=false` against a
'      scratch checkout that persists) to get the upper bound on the saving
'   c. only if (b) is a large multiple of (a) does the state question above
'      become worth answering
'
' If the saving is small, the answer is that clone mode is per-run on purpose
' and this file should say so and stop. If it is large, the shape to pursue is
' probably `sbx env` with a proveo-authored environment file — question 4 — and
' NOT a second durable-clone mechanism inside proveo.
title EXPERIMENT — should the clone outlive the run?

actor "operator" as OP

node "host repository" as REPO <<host>> {
  database "the checkout\nmoves under the clone between runs" as SRC <<db>>
  database "refs/proveo/<sid>/*\nONE NAMESPACE PER RUN today" as REFS <<db>>
}

package "today — a clone per run" as NOW {
  database "run #1 clone\ncold: clone, provision, orient" as C1 <<db>>
  database "run #2 clone\ncold AGAIN" as C2 <<db>>
  database "run #3 clone\ncold AGAIN" as C3 <<db>>
}

package "proposed — one clone per (repo, target)" as SOON {
  database "the agent's workspace\nits branch, its dirty tree, its caches" as WS <<db>>
}

component "staleness\nsilent fork, or discarded work" as STALE <<error>>
component "collision\ntwo runs, one key" as COLLIDE <<error>>
component "state with no lifecycle\nnothing to clean, until there is" as STATE <<error>>
component "sbx env\nthe backend already reuses a named sandbox" as SBXENV <<cloud>>

OP ARROW_MAIN C1
OP ARROW_MAIN C2
OP ARROW_MAIN C3
SRC ARROW_MAIN C1 : clone
SRC ARROW_MAIN C2 : clone
SRC ARROW_MAIN C3 : clone
C1 ARROW_QUEUE REFS
C2 ARROW_QUEUE REFS
C3 ARROW_QUEUE REFS

OP ARROW_MAIN WS : run again, land where you left off
WS ARROW_ERROR STALE
WS ARROW_ERROR COLLIDE
WS ARROW_ERROR STATE
SBXENV ARROW_MAIN WS : the layer this may belong to

note bottom of NOW
  **Cold every time, on purpose.** Nothing
  accumulates and nothing is left to clean up —
  the guarantee is simple because the sandbox is
  ephemeral. The cost is that pass #2 rebuilds a
  world pass #1 already had.
end note

note bottom of WS
  **What reuse is FOR.** The agent resumes on its
  own branch with a tree it recognises, the
  toolchain cache survives, and one ref namespace
  answers "what has this agent done" instead of
  "what did run #7 do".
end note

note bottom of STALE
  **The unanswered question.** The host checkout
  moves underneath. Refresh unasked and the
  agent's uncommitted work can go; never refresh
  and the clone is a silent fork. "Ask" is a
  prompt in front of every run.
end note

note bottom of SBXENV
  **Possibly not proveo's to keep.** 0.42's
  environment files already describe a named
  sandbox with its workspaces, credentials and
  lifecycle, reused across invocations. A second
  mechanism above it duplicates the backend.
end note

note right of OP
  **Measure before building.** Time three
  consecutive runs on ""e2e/samples"", then repeat
  against a hand-made durable clone for the upper
  bound. Only a large multiple makes the state
  questions worth answering.
end note
@enduml
